    "ls|LS17_S6_U_340": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF GONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIG DISTRIBUTION\nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED OUESTION NO. 34O\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2OrhJULY, 2O2t\nDIVERSION OF RIGE PROGURED FOR FOOD SEGURITY FOR ETHANOL\nPRODUGTION\n34O. SHRI V.K.SRIEEKANDAN:\nWill the llllinister of GONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIG\nDtsTRtBUTloN 3lrsfral arr&, urc-+ gk srd-dam Fffl{or d'* be pleased to\nstate:\n(b) whether the Government had committed about three lakh tonnes of\nrice procured by the Food Corporation of lndia for ethanol production, if sot\nthe details thereof; and\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT AND\nGONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIG DISTRIBUTIOT{\n(sADHVr NIRAHJAN JYOTI)\n(a) & (b): lndia imports about 85o/o of its crude oil requirement. Domestic\nbio.fuels like ethanol reduce the nationts dependenee on imported fossil\nfuels; in addition its use reduces vehicular pollutionr increases farmetst\nincome, brings new investment opportunity an rural areas thereby helping\nto achieve goal of Atmanirbhar Bharat.\nEthanol is being produced mainly from molasses/sugarcane and\ndamaged food-grains; but availability of these feedstocks as well as\nethanot production capacity is not suflicient to achieve blending talget\nfixed by the Government. Therefore, with a view to increase production of\nfuel grade ethanot for blending with petrol, Government is encouraging\ndistilleries to produce ethanol from surplus rice available with FCI; and\nmaize.\n._-_--2t-\n(a) whether the Government has allocated 78,OOO tonnes of rice\nprocured for food security purposes to be diverted to ethanol production\ninstead at a subsidised rate of Rs.20 per kg, if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government aims to increase the blending levels to 8.5olo\nin the current year, if so, the details thereofif\n\n-2-\nThe FCI rice is sufficient in stock for food security purposes and it\nwill continue to remaln robust as procurement of paddy/rice at Minimum\nSupport Price (lrtSP) continues at expected levets to meet Publlc\nDistribution System (PDS) requirement. For the period of Decembe/2o2G\nNovembey'2ozlt 78844 MT of FGI rice has been allocated to distilleries @\nRs. 2olkg ex-FGI godown for production of ethanol out of which 22716 Ml\nhas been lifted by distilleries lill 14.7.2021.\n(c): Yes Sir. During the current Ethanol Supply Year (ESY) 2O2O'21lDec'\nNov), the Government has targeted to achieve 8.5% blending of ethanol\nwith petrol. Against the requirement of 332 crore litres ot ethanol for\nachieving 8.5% blending with petrol, contracts have been signed for\nsupplyang about 335 crore litres of ethanol by distilleries to Oi! Martetlng\nGompanies (OMGs) for btending of ethano! with petrol; against which about\n{go crore litres of ethanot have been supplied to oMcs by distilleries till\n12.7.2021 thereby achleving 7.93% blending which is the historically\nhighest figure.",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_341": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF GONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIG DISTRIBUTION\nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION\nLOK SABHA\nUI{STARRED QUESTION 1{O. 34{\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2OrHJULY,2O2{\nBENEEICIARIES UNDER PDS\n34{. SHRI PART}IIIBAN S.R.:\nWill the Minister of GOilSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIG\nDlsTRtBuTIox rquhor arr&, urc sik srffiq; fuf,{q t'* be pleased to state:\nstate:\n(a) the names of the commodities allocated to the States by the Union\nGovernment through Pubtic Distribution system (PDS) during the last three\nyears, the details thereof;\n(b) the number of househotds along with the number of beneficiaries who\nhave received these commodaties through PDS; and\n(c) the rates at which each household receives commodities through\nPDS along with the details of the commodities?\nANSUYER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR M!ilISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT AND\nGONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIG DISTRIBUTION\n(sADHVl NIRANJAN JYOTI)\n(a) to (c): The Targeted Public Distribution System (TPDS) is governed as\nper provisions of the National Food Security Act, 2O{3 (NFSA) which\nprovides for al! lndia coverage of upto 75o/o ol the ruIal population and upto\n5O7o ol the urban population which at Gensus 2O{{ come to 8{.35 crore\npersons, for receiving subsidized foodgrains (Ricer Wheat and\nGoarsegrains). Goverage under the Act is under two categories'\nhouseholds covered under Antyodaya Anna Yoiana (a[Y) and the priority\nhousehotds (PHH) to be identified by the state Governments/ Union\nTerritories (uT) Administrations as per criteria evolved by themr within the\ncoverage limit determined for the state/uT. under the Act, while\nAntyodaya Anna Yoiana (AAY) households, are entitled to 35 kg of\nfoodgrains per family per month, priority households are entitled to 5 kg of\nfoodgrains per person per month at uniform subsidized prices of Rs' 31211\nper kg for rice/ wheat/ coarse grains respectively. The details of allocation\nand offtake of foodgrains in regard to States/UTs from year 2Ol8''19 to\n2O2O-21 under NFSA, are placed at Annexure-!.\n\nAnnexure.I\nAilNEXURE REFERRED TO tN REPLY TO PART (a) to (c) OF UNSTARRED\nQUESTIOil No. 34{ FOR ANSWER ON2O.O7.2O2{ IN THE LOK SABHA\nAllocation and Offtake ol'Foodgrains (Rice and Wheat) from year 2018-19 to 2020-21 under TPDS/NFSA\n(in thousand tons)\nsr\nNo\nStatesruTs\n2018-19 2019-20 2020-21\nAllocation Offtake Allocation Offtake Allocation Offtake\n1 1871.844 1871.844 1871.205 1871.844 2147.362\n2 Arunanchal Pradesh 88.992 87.032 88.992 92.023 88.99233 87.22328\nJ Assam 1695.t32 1691.029 1702.142 1694.324 1553.353\n4 Bihar 5493.861 5 188.923 5493.861 5404.327 5524.33 5183.855\n5 1384.056 1375.528 1384.056 1374.945 1384.056 1375.292\n6 Delhi 450.872 390.997 4s0.873 450.6901 449.4721\nGoa 59.031 59.031 59.045 59.04s s8.17318 55.99979\n8 Gujarat 2379.456 2379.462 1924.614 2r78.396 2011.139\n9 Haryana 795.000 795.000 598.623 795 619.6565\n10 508.021 508.020 5t 1.817 s08.02 507.421'7\nl1 Jharkhand 1739.s14 1698.036 t739.st4 1678.827 1739.514 1760.584\n12 Karnataka 2608.836 2609.932 2608.836 2631.142 2608.801 2370.616\n13 Kerala 1425.049 1410.293 1333.91 5 1334.324 1395.973 1395.549\n14 Madhya Pradesh 3564.469 3418.672 3564.468 3394.007 3184.182\nl5 Maharashtra 460s.192 4048.387 4605.192 4029.097 4605.191 3988.186\n16 Manipur 151.832 152.542 159.122 155.855 162.5984 158.8661\n17 Meghalaya 176.175 177.254 176.298 176.157 176.298 174.6963\n18 Mizoram 65.758 66.259 65.758 67.406 65.7s796 64.66309\n19 Nagaland 138.058 137.450 136.247 138.058 138.1618\n20 Odisha 2207.981 2333.963 2211.582 2224.92 2078.529\n21 Punjab 817.567 870.120 616.006 870.12\n22 Rajasthan 2444.405 2791.572 2843.387 2788.073 2752.219\n23 Sikkim 44.324 44.621 44.324 44.081 44.3241 41.70891\n24 Tamil Nadu 3677.752 3580.906 3677.752 3928.213 3535.626\n25 Telangana 133 8.000 1338.000 1354.597 1253.716\n26 Tripura 271.000 272.314 27t.000 264.550 271.1925 243.0109\n27 502.999 502.653 502.999 502.9992\n....21-\nAnd