    "ls|LS17_S6_U_346": "O.I.H\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULT\nURE AND FARMERS WELF\nARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICU\nLTURE, COOPERATION A\nND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n346\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 20\nTH \nJULY, 2021\n \n \nFUNDS FOR DEVELOPMEN\nT OF AGRICULTURE SEC\nTOR\n \n \n3\n4\n6\n. \nSHRIMATI REKHA VERMA\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष एवं \u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी \nbe pleased to State:\n \n \na\n.\n \nthe details of the funds allocated to Uttar Pradesh for development of the \nagriculture sector during the last five years, scheme\n-\nwise; \n \n \nb\n.\n \nthe current gr\nowth of the agriculture sector;\n \n \nc\n.\n \nthe \npercentage\n \nof growth rate of agriculture sector targeted to be achieved in \norder to double the income of the farmers; and \n \n \nd\n.\n \nthe steps taken by the \nGovernment\n \nto achieve the said growth rate for the \nagriculture sector?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMinister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WEL\nFARE \n \n \nक ृ िष एवं \u0000कसान क\u0000या\nण \nमं\u0000ी\n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH\n \nTOMAR)\n \n \n(a)\n  \nThe details of \nfund\ns\n \nallocated to Uttar Pradesh for the development of\n \nthe \nagriculture\n \nsector \nduring the last five years, scheme\n-\nwise are at Annexure. \n \n \n(b)\n \nThe growth \nrate in Gross Value Added in\n \nAgriculture and Allied Se\nctor during \n2020\n-\n21 was\n \n3\n.\n6 percent a\nt 2011\n-\n12\n \nprices.\n  \n \n \n(c) & (d\n) \n \nThe\n \nGovernment has adopted several developmental programmes, \nschemes, reforms and \npolicies that\n \nfocus on higher incomes for\n \nfarmers. All these \npolicies & programmes are being supported \nby higher budgetary allocations and\n \nnon\n-\nbudgetary financial resources by w\nay of creating Corpus Funds like\n \nMicro Irrigation \nFund\n.\n \nOther major steps include \npromotion \nof 10,000 FPOs\n \nscheme,\n \nsupplementary \nincome transfers under PM\n-\nKISAN,\n \nPradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojna (PMFBY), \nPradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojna\n \n(PMKSY), Increase in Minimum Support Price \n(MSPs) for all Kharif & Rabi Crops, Har Med Par Ped, etc.\n \nThe latest\n \nmajor \nintervention includes\n \n‘Atma Nirbhar Bharat \n–\n \nAgriculture’ which includes creation of \n \nan \nAgricul\ntural Infrastructure Fu\nnd (AIF) worth Rs.\n \n1 lakh crore\n, National Bee and \nHoney Mission and special drive to distribute Kisan Credit Cards. \n \n\n \n \n\nAnnexure to LSUQ No 346 \n \nFunds allotted to \n \nUttar Pradesh  \nunder \nCentrally Spons\nored Schemes of  DA&FW  \nduring  2016\n-\n17 to 2020\n-\n21)\n \n(Rs in Crore)\n \n \nSl. No.\n \nSchemes\n \n2016\n-\n17\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n1\n.\n \nSub\n-\nMission on Agricultural \nMechanization\n \n39.77\n \n43.97\n \n75.00\n \n66.18\n \n66.18\n \n2\n.\n \nRashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana\n \n312.28\n \n440.00\n \n333.55\n \n348.06\n \n402.14\n \n3\n.\n \nNational Food Security Mission\n \n197.53\n \n173.99\n \n192.37\n \n161.07\n \n141.40\n \n4\n.\n \nPer Drop More Crop component of \nPMKSY\n \n69.00\n \n100.00\n \n115.00\n \n100.00\n \n300.00\n \n5\n.\n \nNational Food Security Mission\n-\nOilseeds \nand Oil Palm\n \n16.60\n \n15.60\n \n19.49\n \n9.61\n \n31.98\n \n6\n.\n \nParamparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY)\n \n17.38\n \n12.02\n \n11.46\n \n11.19\n \n10.10\n \n7\n.\n \nSoil Health Management \nScheme\n \n22.68\n \n3.00\n \n14.89\n \n3.64\n \n0.17\n \n8\n.\n \nSoil Health Card Scheme\n \n48.84\n \n44.68\n \n46.39\n \n13.30\n \n27.53\n \n9\n.\n \nRainfed Area Development \n-\nComponent \nof NMSA\n \n19.90\n \n20.00\n \n20.00\n \n34.00\n \n30.00\n \n10\n.\n \nMission for Integrated Development of \nHorticulture \n \n37.27\n \n37.27\n \n67.00\n \n67.00\n \n80.00\n \n11\n.\n \nNational e\n-\nGovernance Plan in \nAgriculture (NeGP\n-\nA)\n \n0.00\n \n8.01\n \n2.39\n \n0.00\n \n1.21\n \n12\n.\n \nSub Mission on Agroforestry (SMAF)\n \n0.00\n \n12.00\n \n4.00\n \n4.00\n \n4.00\n \n13\n.\n \nSub\n-\nMission on Agriculture Extension \n(ATMA)\n \n45.87\n \n63.84\n \n105.69\n \n100.63\n \n125.11\n \n14\n.\n \nSu\nb Mission on Seed and Planting \nM\naterials\n \n186.10\n \n206.06\n \n340.60\n \n387.73\n \n389.04\n \n \n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_347": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 347 \nANSWERED ON 20.07.2021 \n \nELECTRICAL VEHICLE UNDER FAME INDIA SCHEME \n \n347. SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES भारी उद्योग मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details and the number of electric vehicles which have been supported by the \nGovernment and are plying on roads under the Faster Adoption and Manufacturing of (Hybrid &) \nElectric Vehicles Scheme till date since implementation of the scheme,  e-vehicle category-wise \nalong with funds allocation; \n \n(b) the steps being taken by the Government to incentivize installation of more public \ncharging infrastructure; \n \n(c) whether the Government has any plan to promote  installation of public charging \ninfrastructure at or around existing petrol/diesel/CNG pumps and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d) the details of charging stations which are functional in the country, State-wise; \n \n(e) whether the Government has any plan to  incentivize usage and installation of solar power \ncells or other renewable sources of energy for charging vehicles at public and private spaces; and \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR HEAVY INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI KRISHAN PAL GURJAR) \n \n(a):  Under FAME India Scheme (Phase -I & Phase -II), since its implementation i.e. 1 st April, \n2015, total 3.61 Lakh Electric vehicles (e -2W-2.35 Lakh, e -3W-0.2 lakh and e -4W- 1.06 Lakh) \namounting to demand incentive of about Rs 600 Cr have been incentivized as o n 9th July, 2021. \nFurther, 862 Electric buses amounting to demand incentive of about Rs 49 2 Cr have been \ndeployed as on 30th June, 2021. \n \n(b) & (c):  Sir, Phase-II of FAME India Scheme is being implemented for a period of 5 years \nw.e.f. 01st April, 2019 with a total budgetary support of Rs. 10,000 crores.  This phase focusses \non supporting electrification of public & shared transportation and aims to support, through \nsubsidies, approx. 7000 e-Buses, 5 lakh e-3 Wheelers, 55000 e-4 Wheeler Passenger Cars and 10 \nlakh e-2 Wheelers. In addition, creation of charging infrastructure is also supported to address \nrange anxiety among users of electric vehicles. \n \n Contd…..2/- \n\n-2- \n \n \n The Department of Heavy Industry had also sanctioned about 520 Charging Stations/ \nInfrastructure for Rs. 43.4 Crore under Phase -I of FAME -India Scheme.  Department of Heavy \nIndustry has also sanctioned 2,877 Electric Vehicles (EVs) Charging Stations amount ing to Rs \n500 Crore (Approx.) in 68 cities across 25 States/UTs under FAME India (Faster Adoption and \nManufacturing of Hybrid & Electric Vehicles in India) scheme phase II. \n \n(d): The details of charging stations functional in the country for electric vehicles under Faster \nAdoption and Manufacturing of Hybrid and Electric Vehicles (FAME) India Scheme is at \nANNEXURE-I. \n \n(e) & (f):  No, Sir. No separate policy is being implemented to incentivize usage and \ninstallation of solar power cells or other renewab le sources of energy for charging vehicles at \npublic and private spaces. \n \n***** \n \nANNEXURE-I \n \nDetails of Charging Stations installed(As on 06-07-2021) \nCity Charging Stations Highway Charging Stations  \nChandigarh 48 Delhi -Chandigarh 24 \nDelhi 94 Mum-Pune 15 \nJaipur 49 Delhi- Jaipur- Agra 29 \nBangalore 45 Jaipur-Delhi Highway 9 \nRanchi 29   \nLucknow 1   \nGoa 17   \nHyderabad  50   \nAgra 10   \nShimla 7   \nTotal 350  77 \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_348": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 348 \n  ANSWERED ON 20/07/2021 \nINCREASING MAN-DAYS UNDER MGNREGA \n \n348.  SHRI RAVIKUMAR D.: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details and the total number of man-days g oing to be generated in 2021-22 with the \namount allocated to Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee \n(MGNREG) Act; \n(b)  the details of the allocation of fund under MG NREGA during the year 2020-21 State-\nwise, and \n(c)  the steps taken by the Ministry to verify the implementation of the guidelines given by \nthe Government in implementing this scheme?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n \n(a): Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guara ntee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS) is a demand driven wage employment programme  which provide for the enhancement \nof livelihood security of the households in rural a reas of the country by providing at least one \nhundred days of guaranteed wage employment in every  financial year to every household whose \nadult members volunteer to do unskilled manual work.  \n \nSince, Mahatma Gandhi NREGS is a demand driven prog ramme, no target in made for total \nnumber of persondays to be generated in a financial  year. The “agreed to” Labour Budget for \ncurrent financial year 2021-22 is 278.32 persondays . The Ministry does periodic revision of the \n“Agree to” Labour Budget of States/UTs in consultation with States/UTs as per their demand and \nperformance so as to provide work in time to every wage seeker on demand for work. \n \nDuring current financial year 2021-22, (as on 16.07 .2021), a total of 120.49 crore person-\ndays have been generated. States/UTs-wise detail of  persondays generated under Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS during the current financial year 2021 -22 (as on 16.07.2021) is given at \nAnnexure-I.   \n \n(b): Mahatma Gandhi NREGA, Being a demand driven pr ogramme, no State/UT-wise \nfinancial allocation is made. The details of State/ UT-wise fund released by Central Government \nduring the last financial year 2020-21 are given at Annexure-II.   \n \n\n(c): The responsibility of implementation of Mahatm a Gandhi NREGA is vested with the \nGovernment of States /UTs. The Ministry has a compr ehensive system of monitoring and review \nmechanism for Mahatma Gandhi NREGS. Some of the imp ortant elements of the above \nframework are listed below: \n(i) The Ministry regularly reviews the performance of the implementation of Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS in States/UTs through various media vi z., Mid-Term Review, Labour Budget \nmeetings, Labour Budget Revision meetings, programm e review meetings. Central Employment \nGuarantee Council and State Employment Guarantee Co uncils periodically monitor \nimplementation of the programme.   \n \n(ii) National Level Monitors, Common Review Mission s and Officers of the Ministry visit \nStates/UTs at regular interval to review implementa tion of the programme. After the field visits, \nthe findings/shortcomings and recommendations are s hared with the States/UTs for appropriate \naction at their end.  \n \n(iii) Auditing Standards have been issued and State s/UTs have been advised to establish \nindependent Social Audit Units, conduct Social Audi t as per Audit of Scheme Rules and training \nof Village resource persons for conducting Social A udit etc. Internal Audit Teams of the \nDepartment also conduct regular audit.  \n \n(iv) All grievances/ complaints received in the Min istry are forwarded to the concerned \nStates/UTs for taking appropriate action including investigation, as per law.  \n \n(v) Steps have been taken to strengthen transparenc y and accountability which include geo-\ntagging of assets, Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT), N ational electronic Fund Management System \n(NeFMS), Aadhar Based Payment System (ABPS), Softwa re for Estimate Calculation using \nrural rates for Employment (SECURE) and appointment  of Ombudsperson in every district of \nStates/UTs.  \n \n*******  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-I referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.348 \ndated 20.07.2021 \n \nPerson-days generated under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS du ring the FY 2021-22         \n(as on 16.07.2021) \n (Figure in crore) \nS. No.  States/UTs Person-days generated \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 18.35  \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 0.13  \n3 ASSAM 2.74  \n4 BIHAR 7.74  \n5 CHHATTISGARH 6.89  \n6 GOA 0.00114  \n7 GUJARAT 3.00  \n8 HARYANA 0.48  \n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 0.80  \n10 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 0.26  \n11 JHARKHAND 4.45  \n12 KARNATAKA 5.90  \n13 KERALA 0.73  \n14 LADAKH 0.00132  \n15 MADHYA PRADESH 13.72  \n16 MAHARASHTRA 2.26  \n17 MANIPUR 0.03  \n18 MEGHALAYA 0.28  \n19 MIZORAM 0.69  \n20 NAGALAND 0.35  \n21 ODISHA 8.04  \n22 PUNJAB 1.24  \n23 RAJASTHAN 8.62  \n24 SIKKIM 0.07  \n25 TAMIL NADU 7.17  \n26 TELANGANA 9.80  \n27 TRIPURA 1.07  \n28 UTTAR PRADESH 6.93  \n29 UTTARAKHAND 0.46  \n30 WEST BENGAL 8.29  \n31 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR 0.0021  \n32 LAKSHADWEEP 0.00  \n33 PUDUCHERRY 0.009  \n34 \nTHE DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI AND \nDAMAN AND DIU 0.00 \n \n  Total 120.49  \nSource: Management Information System \n  \n\nAnnexure-II referred in reply to part (b) of Lok Sa bha Unstarred Question No.348 \ndated 20.07.2021  \n \nCentral funds released under Mahatma Gandhi NREGA during the financial year 2020-21 \n \n(Rs. In crore) \nS. No. States/UTs Central funds released \n1 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR 4.86  \n2 ANDHRA PRADESH 10365.48  \n3 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 348.95  \n4 ASSAM 2636.07  \n5 BIHAR 6647.26  \n6 CHHATTISGARH 4144.19  \n7 GOA 0.91  \n8 GUJARAT 1513.90  \n9 HARYANA 776.67  \n10 HIMACHAL PRADESH 964.98  \n11 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 1166.63  \n12 JHARKHAND 3490.25  \n13 KARNATAKA 5605.48  \n14 KERALA 4300.32  \n15 LADAKH 22.49  \n16 LAKSHADWEEP 0.00  \n17 MADHYA PRADESH 9225