    "ls|LS17_S6_U_436": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 436 \n ANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2021 \n  \nHORTICULTURE PRODUCTION HUB \n  \n436. SHRI RAJU BISTA: \n \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that the North Bengal region is a horticulture production \nhub with huge potential for pineapples, oranges, mangoes, bananas, litchi, jackfruit, lime, \netc., which are in high demand across the world; \n \n(b)  whether some fruit processing facilities are already in existence in the North Bengal \ndistricts of Darjeeling, Kalimpong; and \n \n(c)  whether the Government is considering to establish a food processing plant in the North \nBengal region given the high quality of fruit productio n and suitable agro -climatic \nconditions, if so, the details thereof? \n  \n ANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n  \n(a).  Yes Sir, North Bengal region is a potential Horticulture Production Hub for various fruit \ncrops namely, pineapple, orange, mango, banana, litchi, jackfruit, lime etc. The major growing \nareas of these fruit crops are: \nFruit Crop Major Growing Areas \nPineapple Jalpaiguri, Cooch behar,  Darjeeling, Uttar Dinajpur \nOrange Darjeeling and Kalimpong \nMango Malda \nBanana Jalpaiguri, Cooch behar, Dinajpur \nLitchi Malda and parts of Dinajpur \nJackfruit Jalpaiguri, Cooch behar, Plain area of Darjeeling \nLime / lemon All the district of North Bengal \n   \n(b) & (c).   Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI ) does not set up food processing \ninfrastructure on its own. However, it provides credit linked financial assistance (capital subsidy) \nin the form of Grants-in-Aid to entrepreneurs for setting up of food processing industries, including \nfor processing of fruits, under component schemes of Pradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana \n(PMKSY). PMKSY is demand driven and financial assistance is provided to the eligible applicants \nselected on the basis of Expression of Interest (EoI) issued from time to time. MoFPI has approved \ntwo projects in Darjeeling District of West Bengal for assistance under Creation/Expansion of \nFood Processing and Preservation Capacities Scheme of PMKSY. Related details are placed at \nAnnexure. \n  \n***** \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( b) AND (c) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 436 FOR ANSWER ON 20 TH JULY, 2021  \nREGARDING “HORTICULTURE PRODUCTION HUB”. \n  \nDetails of projects approved under Creation/Expansion of Food Processing and Preservation \nCapacities Scheme: \n  \n        (Rs. In crore) \nSl. \nNo \nName of the Proje\nct \nDistrict Sector Date of  \n Approval \nProject \nCost \nApproved \nGrant \nReleased \nAmount \nStatus \n  \n1. \nM/s Mahananda  \nFood Pvt. Ltd Darjeeling F&V  \nprocessing 07.12.2018  22.94      4.94     4.77 Completed \n2 M/s Aarson  \nLozenges Factory Darjeeling Consumer  \nProducts 29.05.2019 4.75 1.31 0.66 Under \n Implementation \n  \n \n  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_437": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRYAND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.437 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20THJULY, 2021 \nPradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana in Assam \n \n437. SHRI KRIPANATH MALLAH: \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING मत्स्यपालन, \nपशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a)  the features of the Pradhan Mantri Matsya SampadaYojana (PMMSY); \n \n(b) the details of the funds sanctioned, allocated and utilized under this yojana within the State of Assam so far; \n \n(c)  the details of the target and achievements made so far along with its response; and \n \n(d)  whether it has proved to be a boon for the fishermen within the country including the State of Assam? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \n(DR. L. MURUGAN) \n(a): The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying is implementing a \nflagship scheme namely Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) with an estimated investment of \nRs 20050 crore for a period of 5 years  from FY 2020 -21 to FY 2024 -25 in all States/Union Territories  for \nsustainable and responsible development of fisherie s and aquaculture sector in the country. The PMMSY is \nimplemented as an umbrella scheme with two components namely (a) Central Sector Scheme (CS) Component \nand (b) Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) Component. The Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) Component is \nfurther segregated into Non -beneficiary oriented and beneficiary orientated sub -components/activities under \nthree broad heads such as (a) Enhancement of Production and Productivity, (b) Infrastructure and Post-harvest \nManagement and (c) Fisheries Management and Regulatory Framework. \nPMMSY envisages to address the critical gaps in fish production and productivity, quality, technology, \npost-harvest infrastructure and management, modernization and strengthening of value chain, fisheries expor t \ncompetitiveness, traceability, establishing a robust fisheries management framework.  \n(b) to (d) : During the financial year 2020 -21, the Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal \nHusbandry and Dairying had accorded approval to the fisheries development proposal of Government of Assam \nat total cost of Rs. 110.22 crore under the PMMSY.  Out of the Central financial liability of Rs.51.14 crore, an \namount of Rs. 26.77 crore has been released to the Government of Assam for implementation of the approved \nproposal. The Government of Assam so far has utilized central funds of Rs. 10 crore for implementati on of the \napproved fisheries development project in the State.   \nThe PMMSY aims to create multifarious benefits to fishers, fish farmers and other stakeholders for  \nharnessing of fisheries potential in a sustainable, responsible, inclusive and equitable manner, enhancing of fish \nproduction and productivity, expansion, intensification, diversification, productive utilization of land and water, \nmodernizing and strengthening of fisheries value chain, post -harvest management, doubling fishers and fish \nfarmers incomes, generation of employment  opportunities, social, physical and economic security thereby \nimproving social economic status of fishers, fish farmers and other stake holder in the country.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_438": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 438 \nANSWERED ON 20.07.2021 \n \nFOURTEENTH FINANCE COMMISSION \n \n438. SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU: \n \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the grants promised by the Fourteenth Finance Commission (FFC) are provided to \nthe Gram Panchayats, if so, the amount released and utilised for the State of Andhra Pradesh; \n \n(b) whether cheque drawing-powers of Sarpanch can be withdrawn and handed over to Village \nRevenue Officer, if so, the reasons thereof; \n \n(c) the extent of computerisation completed under projects of Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Yojana \nand the Fourteenth Finance Commission; and \n \n(d) the number of Common Service Centres  set up towards provision of online services in \nvillages and Gram Panchayats, in the State of Andhra Pradesh, more specifically the \nSrikakumar district? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a)     Under the Fourt eenth Finance Commission (FFC) award for the period of 2015 -2020, \ngrants to the tune of Rs. 2,00,292.20 crore were allocated to Gram Panchayats (GPs) constituted \nunder Part IX of the Constitution in 26 States including Andhra Pradesh. From this, a total \namount of Rs. 1,83,248.54 crore (91.49%) was released to the States. The details of allocation \nand release of FFC Grants to the State of Andhra Pradesh is as below: \n \n     (Rs. in crore) \n2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 \nAllocati\non  \nRelea\nse \nAllocati\non \nRelea\nse \nAllocati\non \nRelea\nse \nAllocati\non \nRelea\nse \nAllocati\non \nRelea\nse \n \n934.34 928.4\n1 \n1463.4\n5 \n1454.\n05 \n1686.8\n5 \n1675.\n88 \n1947.3\n2 \n1729.\n23 \n2622.1\n3 \n2336.\n56 \n \n            The State Government of Andhra Pradesh has informed that the entire amount released \nto the State under FFC has been utilized. \n\n(b)  Panchayat, being ‘Local Government’, is a State subject and part of Seventh Schedule \nof the Constitution of India. The financial powers of Sarpanches including the cheque drawing \npowers are governed by the Panchayati Raj Acts and Rules including the financial rules of the \nrespective State Governments.  \n(c)    201679 GPs/ Rural Local Bodies across the country are having computer which include \n4146 GPs for which computers have been provided under the scheme of RGSA. Under FFC \npurchase of computers and accessories as well as cost of AMC in GPs which do not have any \ncomputer have been permitted.  \n \n(d)   As per information furnished by the State Government of Andhra Pradesh, a total of 15004 \nCommon Service Centres (CSCs) have been set up in the State. In Srikakulam District the \nnumber of CSCs is 878.  \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_439": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 439 \n  ANSWERED ON 20/07/2021 \nNATIONAL MOBILE MONITORING SOFTWARE UNDER MGNREGS  \n \n439.  DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI: \nSHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY: \nSHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \nSHRI N. REDDEPPA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government had recently launched t he National Mobile Monitoring Software \n(NMMS) App and Area officer monitoring App, if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the NMMS APP will help in taking real time attendance of workers at Mahatma \nGandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) worksites along \nwith their geo-tagged photograph, if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether this would help in increasing the citi zen oversight of the programme as well as \nenable processing payments faster, if so, the details thereof; \n(d)  whether the Area Officer Monitoring App will f acilitate the officers to record their \nfindings online along with time stamped and go-coordinate tagged photograph for all the \nschemes of Dept of Rural Development, if so, the details thereof; and \n(e)  whether the Government has rolled out any pilo t projects in any of the States to test the \nefficacy of this App, if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a): National Mobile Monitoring Software (NMMS) App  and Area Officer Monitoring App \nwas launched by the Hon’ble Minister of Rural Devel opment on May, 21 2021. These apps are a \nbig step towards bringing the transparency and proper monitoring of the schemes. \n \n(b)&(c):  Yes, Madam; NMMS App permits taking real time attendance of workers at \nMahatma Gandhi NREGA worksites along with geo-tagge d photograph. The app helps in \nincreasing citizen oversight of the programme. \n \n(d): Area Officer Monitoring App was launched in a bid to facilitate the authorities to record \ntheir findings online. It will also help in recordi ng time stamped and go-coordinate tagged \nphotograph for all the schemes of Department of Rur al Development. It facilitates analysis of \nfindings which in turn help in implementation of programme in a better manner. \n \n(e): No pilot study has been conducted. Testing of the App has been done in the field and \nfeedback from States/UTs have been received before its launch for use. \n \n*******  ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_440": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 440\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 20\nTH\n \nJULY, 2021\n \n \nIMPACT OF CHEMICAL FERTILIZERS ON FARMING\n \n \n440\n.\n \nSHRI \nPARTHIBAN S.R.:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)   \nwhether the Government has conducted any study to ascertain the ill effects of use of \nchemical fertilizers in farming; \n \n \n(b)    \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(c)  \nwhether chemical fertilizers contaminate ground water and environment? \n \n \n(d)     \nif so, the details thereof; and \n \n                                                                                         \n \n(e)     \nthe\n \ndetails of steps taken/being taken to protect ground water and environment from \nchemical fertilizers?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष एवं \u0000कसान क\u0000 या\nण मं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a)  \n&\n \n(\nb\n):\n \n    \nThere is no \nscientific evidence of loss of soil fertility\n \nwith judicious use of \nchemical \nfertilizers. However, indiscriminate and imbalanced use of chemical fertilizers \ncoupled with low addition of organic matter and neglect of micro and secondary nutrients over \nyears\n \nmay cause multinutrient deficiencies and deterioration of soil health particularly \nin\n \nintensively cultivated areas.\n \nResults of All India Coordinated Research Project on ‘Long \nTerm Fertilizer Experiments’ over five decades at fixed sites have revealed that\n \ncontinuous \nuse of nitrogenous fertilizer alone had\n \ndeleterious effect on soil health and crop productivity \nshowing deficiencies of other major and micro nutrients. Even with recommended doses of \nNPK, deficiency of micro and secondary nutrients has become \nyield limiting factors over the \nyears.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n(c) & (d): \n   \nThere is possibility of nitrate contamination in groundwater above the permissible \nlimit of 10 mg NO\n3\n-\nN /L due to\n \nexcessive use of nitrogenous fertilizers particularly in light \ntextured soils\n.\n \n \n(e):\n \nSteps taken to protect ground water and environment from chemical fertilizers are \ngiven below:\n \n \n(i)\n \nSoil Health Card Scheme\n \nis implemented to provide soil test based information on \nnutrient status to farmers along with recommendations on appropriate d\nosage of nutrients to \nbe applied for improving soil health and fertility.\n  \nFinancial assistance is provided under Soil \nHealth Card (SHC) for\n \nimparting farmer’s \ntrainings and field demonstrations on balanced use \nof fertilizers.\n \n \n(ii)\n \nThe Indian Council of \nAgricultural Research (ICAR)\n \nis\n \nrecommending soil test based \nbalanced and integrated nutrient management through conjunctive use of both inorganic and \norganic sources (manure, bio\n-\nfertilizers etc.) of plant nutrients with 4Rs approach i.e right \nquantity, r\night time, right mode and right type of fertilizer for judicious use of chemical \nfertilizers to prevent deterioration of soil health and contamination of groundwater. In addition, \nsplit application and placement of fertilizers, use of slow releasing N\n-\nfert\nilizers and nitrification \ninhibitors, growing leguminous crops and use of Resource Conservation Technologies (RCTs) \nare also advocated.\n \n \n \n(iii)\n \nGovernment is focusing on creating awareness and educating the farmers about \norganic farming practices (includin\ng use organic sources of plants nutrients) under the \nParamparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY) and Mission Organic Value Chain Development of \nNorth Eastern Region (MOVCDNER) schemes. \n \n \n(iv) \n \nAwareness generation programs/workshop on various aspects of ground\n \nwater \nincluding preventing ground water pollution and safe use of contaminated water are being \nconducted by\n \nCentral Ground Water Board\n \nperiodically. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n******\n \n \n \n\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_443": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 443 \nANSWERED ON 20.07.2021 \n \nCREATION OF VILLAGE LEVEL ISOLATION CENTRES \n \n443. SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any steps to combat pandemic situation with the help \nof local Panchayati Raj bodies like Gram -Panchayat, Panchayat Samiti and Zilla \nParishad, if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether any additional financial support is proposed for the creation of village level \nisolation centers with necessary infrastructure and facilities; and \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a)     Ministry of Panchayati Raj (MoPR) has been providing technical and advisory support \nto the Panchayats towards taking various steps to combat Covid pandemic situation. The States \nhave been advised to enable Panchayats/ Rural Local Bodies (RLBs) to facilitate sanitation and \nsocial distancing in the rural areas. It has been advised for Panchayats take up effective \ncommunication strategy for spreading awareness involving display boards, dissemination on \nCovid appropriate behavior, mass customized communication SMS/whatsapp messages and \nadherence to appropriate behaviour at the worksites. During the Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyan \n(GKRA) launched by the Government from 20 June, 2020 to 22 October, 2020, the \nimplementing states were advised to utilize the Central Finance Commission Grants in \nconvergence with MGNREGS in order to provide employment to returning migrant labourers \nin construction of Panchayat Bhawans and other public infrastructure.  \n \nMinistry of Health and Family Welfare (MoHFW) has issued a detailed Standard \nOperating Procedure (SOP) dated 16 -5-2021 on ‘Covid 19 Containment and Management in \nperi-urban, rural and tribal areas’ which outlines the containment and clinical management \npractices with respect to Covid – 19 management, The SOP has advocated a multi -pronged \napproach l ed by Gram Panchayats (GPs) and engaging other stakeholders for coordinating \ncommunity action and awareness creation at the village level. MoPR has accordingly advised \nthe states to empower RLBs to implement various instructions contained in the SOP.  In order \nto monitor the various key performance indicators  on real time basis, MoPR has  recently \nlaunched an online dashboard capturing the key indicators on Covid Preparedness and Covid \nResponse in rural areas at different levels of Panchayats. \n \n\n(b) & (c) To enable the Panchayats to effectively fight the Covid pandemic, MoPR has advised \nthe States to enable Gram Panchayats to utilise the Fourteenth Finance Commission (XIV FC) \nunspent balance grants and Fifteenth Finance Commission (XV FC) Basic (Untied) grants  for \nthe purchase of masks, Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) kits, sanitizers, oximeters, \ninfrared thermometers, oxygen cylinders/concentrators  etc, preparation of isolation centre/ \nCovid Care Centre, creation of additional beds in the Covid Care Centre  etc. The expenditure \ninvolving larger outlay s like group insurance premiums, vaccination costs for the village \npopulation, etc were advised to be met from the Finance Commission Grants with the District/ \nBlock Panchayats.  \n \nIn order to provide the necessa ry resources to the Panchayats, the first instalment of \nFifteenth Finance Commission (XV FC) Basic (Untied) Grants and Tied Grants for FY 2020 -\n21 were released by Ministry of Finance (MoF) based on recommendation of MoPR, without \nany pre-conditions to the RLBs. Further, in view of the sudden rise in the Covid pandemic, the \nfirst instalment of XV FC Basic (Untied) Grants of FY 2021-22 to 25 States were also released \nin advance in the month of May, 2021, as a special case, so that these funds could be utilize d \nby the Panchayats for various prevention and mitigation measures to combat the Covid \npandemic. \n \n \n***** \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S6_U_445": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n445\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n20\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2021\n \n \nPROCUREMENT AT MSP\n \n445\n.\n \nSHRI \n \nRAHUL KASWAN:\n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nकल्\n \nयाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe \npleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Minimum Support Price (MSP) for green gram, kidney bean, millet, \ngroundnut, oilseeds was issued but the procurement thereof was not made\n \nby the Government \non time and crops of farmers are rejected in the name of poor quality\n;\n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof, State\n-\nwise\n;\n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government \nproposes to chalk out any action plan for redressal of the \nproblems of farmers\n;\n \nand\n \n \n \n(d)\n \nif\n \nso, the details thereof\n?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nकल् \n \nयाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a) to (b\n):\n \nGovernment\n \nannounces\n \nMinimum Support Prices (MSP)\n \nfor \n \ngreen gram, millet\n \n(jowar, Bajra & Ragi)\n, groundnut and  \nvarious other \noilseeds. However, the MSP of kidney bean \nis not announced by the Government.  Besides MSP\n \nannouncement, Government also \nundertake\ns\n \nprocurement operations of notified crops \nhaving \nFair Average Quality (FAQ)\n \nthrough various designated procuring \nagencies directly\n \nfrom farmers. \n \n \n \n \nMoreover, b\nased on the proposal of the concerned State/UT Govt\n.\n \nunder respective \nSchemes, t\nhe \nGovernment \nensure\ns\n \nprocurement of crops \nconforming to the specifications \nprescribed by Government are purchased at Minimum Support Price (MSP) \ndirectly from \nfarmers through various designated procuring agencies during the harvesting period\n.\n \nThe State \nwise procurement of green gram, millets (\njowa\nr, Bajra & Ragi\n) and oilseeds including ground \nnuts \nsince 2016 \nis attached at \nAnnexure \nI\n.\n \n \n \n \n\n \nThe pulses, oil\nseeds and copra of Fair Average Quality (FAQ) are procured from \nregistered farmers under Price Support Scheme under Umbrella Scheme of Pradhan \nMantri Annadata Aay SanraksHan Abhiyan (PM\n-\nAASHA), as per its prescribed \nguidelines at MSP in consultation with\n \nthe concerned State Government\n. Under PM\n-\nAASHA, States / UTs are \nalso \noffered to choose either Price Support Scheme (PSS) or \nPrice Deficiency Pa\nyment Scheme (PDPS) in a given procurement season with respect \nto particular oilseeds crop for the entire State. Further, States have the option to roll out \nPrivate Procurement and Stockist Scheme (PPSS) on pilot basis in district / selected \nAPMC(s) of dis\ntrict involving the participation of\n \nprivate stockist for oilseeds.\n \n \n \n \n \nThe millets \nare procured by State Governments itself in consultation with FCI to \nthe extent that the concerned State Government may utilise the same for distribution \nunder Targeted Pub\nlic Distribution System (TPDS) as well as Other Welfare Schemes \n(OWS).\n \n \n \n(c) to (d) :   \nSince Central Nodal Agencies undertake \nMSP operation on behalf of \nGovernment of India \ndirectly from farmers through state designated procuring agencies\n, \nthe  redressal \nof the problems of farmers is dealt by the \nconcerned \nState Government\n \n \nagencies\n \nas per existing norms of the Schemes\n.\n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nto LSQ No. 445\n \n \nDetails of Procurement of Moong, Oilseeds and Millets  (Jowar, Bajra and Ragi) procured at \nMSP since 2016\n-\n17\n \n \n(as on 11.07.2021)\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nQty. In MT\n \n \nYear/States\n \nMOONG\n \nGROUNDNUT \nMUSTARD \nSEED \n \nNIGER\n \nSEED\n \nSESAMUM \nSEED \n \nSOYABEAN \nSUNFLOWER \nSEED \n \nJOWAR\n \nBAJRA\n \nRAGI\n \n2016\n-\n1